## The residence permit for retirees

Mauritius offers a residence permit to retirees aged **50 or older**. The Economic Development Board's conditions:

- A bank statement from your home country showing at least **USD 24,000**, or a guaranteed income of **USD 2,000 a month**.
- Once the permit is issued, an initial transfer of at least USD 2,000 to a Mauritian bank within 60 days, then USD 24,000 a year or USD 2,000 a month.
- The permit is valid for **ten years** and renewable for ten more. The fee is USD 1,000, and since 1 December 2025 there is also a non refundable application fee of USD 50.
- Your spouse, children and parents who depend on you can apply for a permit for the same period.

You may invest in any business, as long as you are not employed in it and take no salary from it.

## Permanent residence after five years

After five years as a retired resident, and with at least **USD 200,000** transferred in total over those five years, you can apply for a **Permanent Residence Permit**, valid for twenty years and renewable.

## Applying

The application starts online on the EDB's licensing platform; you do not need to be in Mauritius until the approval in principle. You will need, among other things, your birth certificate, passport, a certificate of character covering ten years and recent photographs, then later a Mauritian bank statement and a medical certificate.

## Buying a home

Retirees can buy under the [PDS](/en/living-in-the-west/pds), [IRS](/en/living-in-the-west/irs), [RES](/en/living-in-the-west/res) or [Smart City](/en/living-in-the-west/smart-city) schemes, or an apartment in a building of at least ground plus two floors. A home of USD 375,000 or more gives its own residence permit. To try a long stay before buying, look at the one year renewable [Premium Visa](https://residency.mu/live/mauritius-premium-visa/) made for stays of up to a year, renewable, without working in Mauritius.

## Tax

You become tax resident if you stay 183 days or more in a year, or 270 days over the current and previous year. The EDB notes that foreign income, including pensions, is taxable for residents, and that the treatment of a pension depends on the tax treaty with your country and on whether it is brought into Mauritius. Take advice before you move.

## Where to retire on the west coast

- **[Flic en Flac](/en/areas/flic-en-flac)**: one of the longest beaches on the island, lively at weekends and relaxed the rest of the week.
- **[Tamarin](/en/areas/tamarin)**: a village on a wide bay ringed by mountains, with dolphins in the early morning.
- **[Black River](/en/areas/black-river)**: the working heart of the coast, with La Preneuse beach and the national park on the doorstep.
- **[La Gaulette](/en/areas/la-gaulette)**: a small, unhurried village between the mountains and a wide lagoon.
- **[Le Morne](/en/areas/le-morne)**: some of the finest beaches on the island, below the UNESCO listed mountain.
- **[Chamarel](/en/areas/chamarel)**: a village on a wooded plateau about 260 metres up, with cooler air.

Each area page has a Living section with what daily life is like.

*Rules and amounts change. Everything here was checked against the Economic Development Board's own pages on 2 October 2026; confirm the details with the EDB, your notary and the developer before you sign.*
